WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.567
RoBERTa.

2
00:00:01.867 --> 00:00:05.028
It beat BERT everywhere without changing a single layer.

3
00:00:05.028 --> 00:00:06.100
So what did change?

4
00:00:07.300 --> 00:00:09.533
RoBERTa is BERT with the training done right.

5
00:00:09.833 --> 00:00:11.433
The architecture is the same.

6
00:00:11.733 --> 00:00:14.433
Only the recipe improved, and that was enough.

7
00:00:15.633 --> 00:00:16.600
First, data.

8
00:00:16.900 --> 00:00:19.534
BERT trained on sixteen gigabytes of text.

9
00:00:19.534 --> 00:00:24.300
RoBERTa used one hundred sixty, and trained much longer with bigger batches.

10
00:00:25.500 --> 00:00:26.667
Two more changes.

11
00:00:26.967 --> 00:00:32.700
Masking became dynamic: a new random pattern each pass, instead of one frozen pattern.

12
00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:36.800
And the next-sentence objective was dropped, because it proved useless.

13
00:00:37.100 --> 00:00:40.333
The lesson: recipe matters as much as architecture.

14
00:00:41.533 --> 00:00:43.100
Here is a fair experiment.

15
00:00:43.400 --> 00:00:45.842
One line changes: the checkpoint name.

16
00:00:45.842 --> 00:00:49.633
Distilbert scores point eight nine, RoBERTa point nine two.

17
00:00:49.933 --> 00:00:54.700
Everything else is identical, because the Auto classes make checkpoint swaps free.

18
00:00:55.900 --> 00:00:58.133
Here is the lesson in a real project.

19
00:00:58.433 --> 00:01:04.467
For an anomaly classifier, you run the identical fine-tune on three backbones and log every score.

20
00:01:04.767 --> 00:01:08.667
RoBERTa wins by three points, which is worth it for batch triage.

21
00:01:08.967 --> 00:01:13.433
But on the latency-critical path, distilbert is twice as fast, so it stays.

22
00:01:14.633 --> 00:01:19.867
Be suspicious of comparisons that change both architecture and recipe at once.

23
00:01:20.167 --> 00:01:25.067
Before redesigning anything, ask whether more epochs or better data closes the gap.

24
00:01:26.267 --> 00:01:27.059
Let's recap.

25
00:01:27.059 --> 00:01:29.500
RoBERTa is BERT with a better recipe.

26
00:01:29.800 --> 00:01:34.267
Ten times the data, dynamic masking, no next-sentence objective.

27
00:01:34.567 --> 00:01:37.100
Recipe matters as much as architecture.

28
00:01:37.400 --> 00:01:40.879
So benchmark a few backbones before committing.

29
00:01:40.879 --> 00:01:41.767
Next up: T5.
